item.text:
Searches covered Russian presidential/legal records, Ukrainian negotiation communications, UN/OHCHR accountability findings, OSCE command-and-control findings, and later official admissions specific to 2022 Wagner financing. The strongest recoveries are Putin’s signed mobilization decree and OHCHR’s defined accountability review. For negotiations, official Ukrainian communications establish proposals and unresolved conditions, but the complete contemporaneous Russian/Ukrainian marked-up draft texts remain inaccessible except through later journalistic reproductions. No reliable evidence was found separating 2022 military expenditure into Putin-linked personal enrichment; expenditure should therefore support escalation only. No specific 2022 Russian senior remedial action concerning affiliated-force abuses was found.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Decree of the President of the Russian Federation No. 647, On the Announcement of Partial Mobilization in the Russian Federation","publisher":"Rossiyskaya Gazeta / President of the Russian Federation","publication_date":"2022-09-21","url":"https://rg.ru/documents/2022/09/21/prezident-ukaz647-site-dok.html","claim":"Putin signed Decree No. 647 personally announcing partial mobilization from 21 September 2022, ordering conscription into the Russian Armed Forces, directing federal financing and supply measures, and requiring regional leaders to meet Defence Ministry quotas and deadlines. This directly attributes the escalation decision to Putin.","locator":"Decree heading and signature; clauses 1-3, 6, 8 and 10; page lines 57-87","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-001","canonical_fact_key":"putin_partial_mobilization_decree_2022_09_21","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.8"],"source_type":"official_presidential_decree","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Officially published, signed presidential legal act with operative provisions and effective date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct contemporaneous decision on 21 September 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct: the decree is signed by President Vladimir Putin and exercises presidential statutory authority.","contrary_evidence":"The decree calls the measure partial but does not publicly specify the overall numerical ceiling; its classified clause 7 prevents full scrutiny of scope.","underlying_fact_key":"putin_partial_mobilization_decree_2022_09_21","lenses":["2B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"During the negotiations with Russia, the Ukrainian delegation officially outlined its proposals for a new system of security guarantees","publisher":"Office of the President of Ukraine","publication_date":"2022-03-29","url":"https://www.president.gov.ua/en/news/na-peregovorah-iz-rosiyeyu-ukrayinska-delegaciya-oficijno-pr-73933","claim":"Ukraine's delegation publicly described its Istanbul proposals—permanent neutrality in exchange for a ratified multilateral security-guarantee treaty—but expressly said no documents had been signed. Territorial questions remained outside the proposed guarantees, guarantor participation still required discussion, negotiations were to continue, and implementation required a Ukrainian referendum and parliamentary ratification.","locator":"Paragraphs corresponding to page lines 201-215 and 217, especially the no-signature statement at line 203 and unresolved implementation requirements at lines 205, 208 and 215-217","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-002","canonical_fact_key":"ukraine_istanbul_proposals_not_signed_2022_03_29","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.7"],"source_type":"official_negotiating_party_communication","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous statement from Ukraine's presidency reporting its own delegation's proposals and their procedural status.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Issued on the day of the Istanbul negotiating session.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual to Putin: it establishes what Russia received from the Ukrainian side, but does not by itself prove Putin accepted or rejected each term.","contrary_evidence":"The proposals show meaningful Ukrainian openness to neutrality and Russian participation as a guarantor; however, the explicit absence of signatures and unresolved guarantor, territorial, referendum and ratification questions contradict characterization as a mutually accepted final settlement.","underlying_fact_key":"ukraine_istanbul_proposals_not_signed_2022_03_29","lenses":["2B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Mykhailo Podolyak: Ukraine's position in negotiations with Russia will depend on success on the frontline","publisher":"Office of the President of Ukraine","publication_date":"2022-04-11","url":"https://www.president.gov.ua/en/news/mihajlo-podolyak-poziciyi-ukrayini-na-peregovorah-z-rosiyeyu-74265","claim":"Ukraine still treated the Istanbul Communiqué as its negotiating position on 11 April, but insisted territorial integrity and sovereignty were inviolable, sought enforceable multilateral rather than merely bilateral guarantees, and stated that its negotiating position would depend on battlefield developments and President Zelenskyy's judgment. This shows negotiations continued without mutual acceptance of all settlement terms.","locator":"Page lines 200-205 and 208-212","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-003","canonical_fact_key":"ukraine_istanbul_position_conditions_2022_04_11","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.7"],"source_type":"official_negotiating_party_communication","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous official communication quoting a senior Ukrainian presidential negotiator about Ukraine's position.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly describes the negotiating position on 11 April 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual to Putin; it identifies unresolved Ukrainian conditions against which any claimed Russian acceptance must be tested.","contrary_evidence":"It confirms that a live communiqué and negotiations existed, contrary to claims that no serious negotiating channel existed; it simultaneously shows that decisive sovereignty, guarantee and battlefield-dependent issues remained unresolved.","underlying_fact_key":"ukraine_istanbul_position_conditions_2022_04_11","lenses":["2B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Statement on the second OSCE Moscow Mechanism report on Ukraine","publisher":"Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe","publication_date":"2022-07-14","url":"https://cdn.osce.org/sites/default/files/f/documents/b/7/524706.pdf","claim":"The second Moscow Mechanism mission documented a consistent pattern of abduction, interrogation, mistreatment and sometimes killing in areas occupied by Russian forces. It could not determine whether the conditions resulted from a coordinated plan or troop disorganization, but concluded Russia remained responsible even for ultra vires acts and had a duty to investigate and hold individual perpetrators responsible.","locator":"PDF page 1, lines 58-71, especially lines 66-71","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-004","canonical_fact_key":"osce_russian_occupation_abuse_control_and_duty_to_investigate_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.5","2B.9"],"source_type":"official_intergovernmental_fact_finding_summary","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official OSCE record quoting the findings of an independent Moscow Mechanism expert mission, with an explicit uncertainty limitation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Mission examined conduct from 1 April through 25 June 2022 and reported in July 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Institutional rather than personal: Putin headed the state and armed forces, but this source does not establish his knowledge of each incident or a personal order.","contrary_evidence":"The mission expressly could not distinguish a coordinated plan from disorganization, so the evidence does not prove Putin directed the individual abuses; it does establish state responsibility and a remedial duty.","underlying_fact_key":"osce_russian_occupation_abuse_control_and_duty_to_investigate_2022","lenses":["2B.5","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Killings of civilians: summary executions and attacks on individual civilians in Kyiv, Chernihiv, and Sumy regions","publisher":"Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights","publication_date":"2022-12-07","url":"https://www.ohchr.org/sites/default/files/2022-12/2022-12-07-OHCHR-Thematic-Report-Killings-EN.pdf","claim":"After reviewing Russian domestic criminal provisions and the large volume of public information implicating Russian armed forces in civilian killings, OHCHR found no information that Russian authorities were actively investigating or prosecuting any such case.","locator":"Accountability section, paragraph 83, PDF page 19","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-005","canonical_fact_key":"ohchr_no_identified_russian_investigation_civilian_killings_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.9"],"source_type":"un_human_rights_investigative_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"A defined UN monitoring finding tied to a stated evidentiary base, specified offence categories and Russian criminal-law provisions; it is more checkable than an unrestricted assertion that no investigation existed.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in December 2022 about killings and accountability during the 2022 invasion.","ruler_attribution":"Supports state-level non-remedy under Putin's presidency and supreme command, but does not independently prove that Putin personally blocked a named investigation.","contrary_evidence":"This is a carefully bounded absence finding—OHCHR found no information—rather than proof that no confidential or unpublicized proceeding existed.","underlying_fact_key":"ohchr_no_identified_russian_investigation_civilian_killings_2022","lenses":["2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Killings of civilians: summary executions and attacks on individual civilians in Kyiv, Chernihiv, and Sumy regions","publisher":"Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights","publication_date":"2022-12-07","url":"https://www.ohchr.org/sites/default/files/2022-12/2022-12-07-OHCHR-Thematic-Report-Killings-EN.pdf","claim":"OHCHR concluded that Russian forces' repeated failures to respect rules governing hostilities and persons in their power indicated deficient instructions and training, ineffective command and control, and an insufficient disciplinary system. It could identify no Russian soldier or commander held accountable for carrying out or failing to punish the documented killings and recommended clear orders to armed forces, affiliated armed groups and private military personnel.","locator":"Conclusions and recommendations, paragraphs 88-91, PDF page 21, especially paragraphs 88, 89 and recommendation 91(e)","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-006","canonical_fact_key":"ohchr_russian_command_control_discipline_and_nonremedy_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.5","2B.9"],"source_type":"un_human_rights_investigative_report","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specific UN findings and recommendations grounded in documented 2022 cases, with separate treatment of command systems, accountability and affiliated personnel.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Contemporaneous December 2022 assessment of conduct from February-April 2022 and accountability through the reporting cutoff.","ruler_attribution":"Establishes institutional failure under Putin's supreme command and the absence of an identified Russian accountability case; personal knowledge, deliberate non-remedy or an individual Putin instruction is not independently demonstrated.","contrary_evidence":"The finding points partly to ineffective control and discipline, which can indicate organizational failure rather than a proven centrally coordinated policy for every abuse.","underlying_fact_key":"ohchr_russian_command_control_discipline_and_nonremedy_2022","lenses":["2B.5","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Guns for gold: the Wagner Network exposed","publisher":"House of Commons Foreign Affairs Committee","publication_date":"2023-07-26","url":"https://publications.parliament.uk/pa/cm5803/cmselect/cmfaff/167/report.html","claim":"The committee found that Russia's 2022 invasion confirmed Wagner operated with extensive Russian Defence Ministry support and cooperation, was primarily state-funded, and received direct government facilitation despite its nominally illegal arm's-length status. Evidence included weapons, ammunition, logistics and transport supplied from Russian military resources.","locator":"Report paragraphs 3, 5 and 8; page lines 28-34 and 40-43; evidentiary qualifications in paragraphs 2 and 9-10","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-007","canonical_fact_key":"russian_state_support_and_facilitation_of_wagner_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.5"],"source_type":"legislative_inquiry_report","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Substantive parliamentary inquiry using commissioned research and witness evidence, but published later and partly reliant on confidential or retrospective testimony.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Later report specifically establishing Wagner's support and operations during Russia's 2022 invasion.","ruler_attribution":"Shows Russian state and Defence Ministry support under Putin, not that Putin exercised tactical control over every Wagner unit or personally authorized a particular abuse.","contrary_evidence":"The report describes Wagner as sprawling and decentralized and acknowledges uncertainty around membership and deployments; state support is stronger evidence than incident-level effective control.","underlying_fact_key":"russian_state_support_and_facilitation_of_wagner_2022","lenses":["2B.5"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Guns for gold: the Wagner Network exposed","publisher":"House of Commons Foreign Affairs Committee","publication_date":"2023-07-26","url":"https://publications.parliament.uk/pa/cm5803/cmselect/cmfaff/167/report.html","claim":"The committee recorded Putin's later admission that the Russian state fully financed Wagner, including 86.262 billion rubles in maintenance and incentive payments from May 2022 to May 2023. This specifically confirms state financial ownership of Wagner's capacity during most of calendar 2022, while not establishing Putin's personal enrichment.","locator":"Findings on state funding in paragraphs 3 and 5; underlying Putin admission discussed in the report's funding evidence and footnotes; distinguish from Prigozhin-linked commercial benefits described in paragraph 3","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C1-008","canonical_fact_key":"putin_admission_state_financing_wagner_from_may_2022","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.5","2B.8"],"source_type":"legislative_inquiry_citing_ruler_admission","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"The financial proposition derives from Putin's recorded admission and is reproduced by a parliamentary inquiry, but the cited total spans May 2022-May 2023 and is not disaggregated by calendar year.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The admitted payment interval begins in May 2022 and therefore directly covers eight months of the target year, though the total cannot be assigned wholly to 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct admission by Putin concerning state financing; it supports resourcing and tolerance of Wagner, not personal receipt of the funds.","contrary_evidence":"The record separates Wagner maintenance and incentive payments from Prigozhin-related commercial interests; it does not show that Putin personally profited or that all military expenditure constituted patronage or enrichment.","underlying_fact_key":"putin_admission_state_financing_wagner_from_may_2022","lenses":["2B.5","2B.8"]}
